TTYCHATTER C COMMAND-LINE WALKTHROUGH
=====================================

This walkthrough guides a first-time user from an empty setup to a resumed
session with attachments.  It is written for the C command-line version, not
the ncurses or Bash clients.

1. Build the program
--------------------

Install development packages on Debian, Ubuntu, or ChromeOS Linux:

    sudo apt-get update
    sudo apt-get install build-essential pkg-config libcurl4-openssl-dev libjson-c-dev ca-certificates gnupg

Build:

    cd c
    make

Check that the binary runs:

    ./ttychatter --version
    ./ttychatter --help

2. Store your OpenRouter API key
--------------------------------

Encrypted storage is recommended:

    ./ttychatter --set-api-key --gpg

You will paste the API key, then GPG will ask for encryption/passphrase handling
according to your local GPG setup.

If you prefer environment variables, use:

    export OPENROUTER_API_KEY="your-key"

Plaintext config storage is also available:

    ./ttychatter --set-api-key

3. Check local diagnostics
--------------------------

Run:

    ./ttychatter --doctor

The doctor command tells you whether ttychatter found your key, cache paths,
GPG, and libcurl.  It does not print the API key itself.

4. Get the model list
---------------------

Refresh the OpenRouter model cache:

    ./ttychatter --update-models

List cached models:

    ./ttychatter --models

Search cached models:

    ./ttychatter --models --search llama
    ./ttychatter --models --model-type routers
    ./ttychatter --models --model-type free

5. Test a model
---------------

Test one model before starting a real session:

    ./ttychatter --test-model openrouter/auto

If the model replies, you can use it for a session.

6. Create your first input file
-------------------------------

Use any editor or shell tool:

    cat > first.txt
    Explain the difference between a router model and a fixed model.
    ^D

7. Generate output into a session file
--------------------------------------

Run:

    ./ttychatter -m openrouter/auto first.txt session.log

Open the session log:

    less session.log

8. Resume the session
---------------------

Create another input file:

    cat > followup.txt
    Explain that again using a short Unix analogy.
    ^D

Reuse the same output file:

    ./ttychatter -m openrouter/auto followup.txt session.log

The program reads recent turns from session.log and appends a new turn.

9. Attach files
---------------

Attach one text file:

    ./ttychatter -a notes.txt question.txt session.log

Attach multiple files:

    ./ttychatter -a notes.txt -a outline.md -a diagram.png question.txt session.log

Text attachments are inserted into the prompt.  Image attachments are sent as
OpenRouter chat content parts when the selected model supports image input.
Other binary files are listed but not fully embedded in this first C CLI release.

10. Use loopback for pipes
--------------------------

To print the AI response to stdout as well as append it to the session log:

    ./ttychatter --loopback question.txt session.log | tee latest-response.txt

This makes ttychatter fit classic shell pipelines while still preserving the
session file.
